`]}),e.jsxs(n,{title:"A sigmoid gate on each head's output",children:[e.jsxs(t.p,{children:["Under the softmax explanation, a head with nothing to write parks its spare weight on a position whose value changes the output little. Gated attention gives each head a direct way to write little. It multiplies the head's attention output by a sigmoid gate, placed after scaled dot-product attention (SDPA) and before the output projection ",e.jsx(a,{id:"qiu2025gated",children:"Qiu et al., 2025"}),"."]}),e.jsxs(t.p,{children:["In the slide's formula, ",e.jsx(s,{math:"h_t \\in \\mathbb{R}^{D}",display:!1})," is the normalized hidden state of token ",e.jsx(s,{math:"t",display:!1})," at the input of the attention block. The output ",e.jsx(s,{math:"o_{t,h} \\in \\mathbb{R}^{D_h}",display:!1})," of head ",e.jsx(s,{math:"h",display:!1})," is taken at position ",e.jsx(s,{math:"t",display:!1})," before the heads are stacked. The head's learned gate projection is ",e.jsx(s,{math:"W^G_h \\in \\mathbb{R}^{D_h \\times D}",display:!1,trail:","})," ",e.jsx(s,{math:"\\sigma",display:!1})," is the logistic sigmoid applied to each coordinate, and ",e.jsx(s,{math:"W_O",display:!1})," is the output projection applied to the stacked head outputs. Each gate value lies between 0 and 1 and scales one coordinate of one head's output, so a value near 0 switches that coordinate off for this token. The gate depends on the current token alone, so like the learned sink bias it adds nothing to the KV cache."]}),e.jsxs(t.p,{children:["For fixed attention weights, a head's value projection and the output projection merge into one linear map. The gate puts a nonlinearity between them, which the authors consider a likely source of its gain. The gate also adds sparsity that depends on the query. It is computed from the current query's hidden state, so what it switches off changes from one query to the next. Most gate values are small ",e.jsx(a,{id:"qiu2025gated",children:"Qiu et al., 2025"}),"."]}),e.jsxs(t.p,{children:["In 15B mixture-of-experts models trained on 400B tokens, the elementwise gate cut the share of attention on the first token, averaged over layers, from 46.7% to 4.8%. In a 1.7B dense model trained on 3.5T tokens, it largely removed the loss spikes. The authors' explanation for the vanishing sink is a hypothesis. The gate can filter out context irrelevant to the current query, so a head with almost nothing to write scales its output toward zero instead of parking weight on the first token. A gate after the value projection reduced the massive activations but not the attention on the first token. The authors conclude that massive activations are not a necessary condition for attention sinks ",e.jsx(a,{id:"qiu2025gated",children:"Qiu et al., 2025"}),"."]}),e.jsx(o,{title:"The recommended variant and released models",children:e.jsxs(t.p,{children:["The paper recommends this elementwise form over a headwise variant with one scalar per head ",e.jsx(a,{id:"qiu2025gated",children:"Qiu et al., 2025"}),". Qwen3-Next takes the paper's most effective variant, the SDPA output gate ",e.jsx(a,{id:"qiu2025gated",children:"Qiu et al., 2025"}),". A.X K2 applies a gate to the output of each head throughout pre-training ",e.jsx(a,{id:"baek2026axk2",children:"Baek et al., 2026"}),". Arcee Trinity Large gates every coordinate of the attention output before the output projection and names the reduction of loss spikes as a key reason for adopting it ",e.jsx(a,{id:"arcee2026trinitylarge",children:"Singh et al., 2026"}),". Week 8's Gated DeltaNet also uses a gate, in a different place: it gates a recurrent state instead of an attention output."]})})]})]}),e.jsxs(i,{title:"Compressed KV entries: CSA, HCA, CSA2",optional:!0,accent:5,number:"47",children:[e.jsx(A,{}),e.jsxs(t.ul,{children:[`
