`]}),e.jsxs(n,{title:"Three ways DeepSeek shrank the cache",children:[e.jsxs(t.p,{children:["The widget redraws Figure 1b of the DeepSeek-V4.1-Flash report, which plots the global KV cache in bytes per token, summed over all layers that keep one ",e.jsx(a,{id:"deepseekai2026deepseekv41",children:"DeepSeek-AI, 2026b"}),". From the first bar to the last the factor is ",e.jsx(s,{math:"389{,}120 / 890 \\approx 437.2",display:!1,trail:","})," which the report's caption rounds to 437-fold ",e.jsx(a,{id:"deepseekai2026deepseekv41",children:"DeepSeek-AI, 2026b"}),". The four bars are four different models that differ in size, depth and much else, so the figure is no controlled ablation and no single step isolates one mechanism."]}),e.jsx(t.p,{children:"Between the bars, three mechanisms changed what is stored. V3.2 stores a narrow MLA entry, with the latent in FP8, in place of V1's per-head keys and values. V4-Flash keeps fewer entries along the sequence because CSA and HCA merge tokens. V4.1-Flash keeps fewer entries across layers because most of its layers reuse an earlier layer's entries."}),e.jsxs(t.p,{children:["DeepSeek-V4, the next model after V3.2, keeps DSA's selection ",e.jsx(a,{id:"deepseekv4",children:"DeepSeek-AI, 2026a"}),". In CSA, a trained compressor merges the key-value entries of every 4 tokens into one entry. The lightning indexer and top-k selection from DSA then choose which compressed entries each query reads. Heavily Compressed Attention (HCA) merges every 128 tokens into one entry and attends to every earlier entry, with no indexer. CSA and HCA layers are interleaved, and each adds an uncompressed sliding window over the most recent 128 tokens. A query attends only to the compressed entries of earlier groups. Without the window it could not reach the earlier tokens of its own group, which is not yet compressed ",e.jsx(a,{id:"deepseekv4",children:"DeepSeek-AI, 2026a"}),". Compression changes what the cache stores, and CSA's top-k changes which stored entries each query reads."]}),e.jsxs(t.p,{children:["DeepSeek-V4.1-Flash replaces V4's mix of CSA and HCA with CSA2 alone, which reuses the main KV entries, the indexer keys and the top-k indices across layers ",e.jsx(a,{id:"deepseekai2026deepseekv41",children:"DeepSeek-AI, 2026b"}),"."]}),e.jsx(o,{title:"V4.1-Flash's layers and storage formats",children:e.jsxs(t.p,{children:["A Full layer computes and stores new entries and selects a fresh top-k. A Reindex layer reuses a preceding layer's entries and indexer keys and selects its own top-k. A Reuse layer reuses both the entries and the top-k indices. Of V4.1-Flash's 40 layers, the first two use only the sliding window and keep no global entries. The other 38 are 4 Full, 4 Reindex and 30 Reuse layers, so 34 layers reuse the entries of 4 ",e.jsx(a,{id:"deepseekai2026deepseekv41",children:"DeepSeek-AI, 2026b"}),". V4.1-Flash stores its main KV entries in FP4, a 4-bit format with one 8-bit scale for every 16 values, while its sliding-window cache stays in FP8 ",e.jsx(a,{id:"deepseekai2026deepseekv41",children:"DeepSeek-AI, 2026b"}),"."]})}),e.jsx(t.p,{children:'"Global" leaves out two caches. One is the sliding-window cache that V4 and V4.1 keep beside the compressed entries. The other is the cache of any multi-token prediction layers, extra layers that predict tokens further ahead than the next one.'})]})]})]}),`
